<h1>Git Status Frame</h1>
<p>A suite is code, and code lives in a repository. When several people work on the same suite, or when an
	<a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/">AI agent</a> pushes new tests overnight, the copy a controller has
	loaded can fall behind what the repository holds, and a run then exercises old tests without anyone noticing.
	The opposite happens too: a suite edited on the controller's machine and not yet pushed runs tests nobody else
	has. The <strong>Git Status</strong> panel, in the right pane below the configuration, answers both questions
	for every repository a loaded item comes from. It compares each checkout with the <code>main</code> branch of
	its remote and shows the result as one row per repository.</p>
<p>The panel appears when a loaded suite, group or test sits inside a git repository. Load the tutorial suite
	together with two more suites, each cloned from its own repository, and the panel shows three rows. The two
	extra suites here, Demo and Example, are internal ones used only for this demonstration and are not provided
	with Orcraft; any suites of your own in their own repositories show the same way:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/git-status-frame/git-status-three-repos.png", "Git Status panel with three rows: orcraft-test-suite-tutorial in green reading Up to date with remote origin/main, orcraft-test-suite-demo in yellow reading Behind remote origin/main with a pull icon and the remote and local hashes, and orcraft-test-suite-example in yellow reading Ahead of remote origin/main with the two hashes", "large") }}
<p>Each row reads from left to right: a coloured bar and the repository name, a refresh icon, the status chip,
	and what the two sides are at. The status is read when the item is loaded and again when the refresh icon is
	clicked; it contacts the remote to learn where <code>main</code> is, so it needs network access to the
	repository's host.</p>
<ul>
	<li><strong>Up to date with remote origin/main.</strong>, in green, means the checkout is at the remote's
		<code>main</code>. The row shows the subject of that commit. A run now exercises the suite as the repository
		holds it.</li>
	<li><strong>Behind remote origin/main.</strong>, in yellow, means someone has pushed to <code>main</code> since
		the checkout was last updated. The row shows the <strong>Remote Hash</strong> the branch is at and the
		<strong>Local Hash</strong> the checkout is at, and a pull icon appears after the refresh icon.</li>
	<li><strong>Ahead of remote origin/main.</strong>, in the same yellow, means the checkout holds commits that are
		not on <code>main</code>. The suite on this machine is not what the repository publishes, which is worth
		knowing before a result is shared. There is nothing to pull, so the row has no pull icon.</li>
</ul>
<p>When a repository tags its releases, the tags take the place of the hashes. A folder that is not a git
	checkout, or whose remote cannot be reached, shows <strong>Unable to access git repo.</strong> in grey.</p>

<h2 id="the-collapsed-panel">The collapsed panel</h2>
<p>Click the arrow before the <strong>Git Status</strong> title to fold the panel into one line:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/git-status-frame/git-status-collapsed.png", "Collapsed Git Status panel showing its title and a yellow chip reading 2 of 3 repos not up to date with remote", "large") }}
<p>The collapsed chip summarizes the rows: green when every repository is up to date, and yellow with a count of
	the ones that are not. It keeps the state of the lab's suites visible while the rest of the right pane takes
	the room. Click the arrow again to expand the rows.</p>

<h2 id="pulling-from-the-controller">Pulling from the controller</h2>
<p>Click the pull icon on the Demo suite's row. The panel asks before touching the checkout:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/git-status-frame/confirm-pull-dialog.png", "Confirm Pull dialog asking whether to pull the latest changes from origin/main and fetch tags for orcraft-test-suite-demo, with Yes and No buttons", "small") }}
<p>Confirm with <strong>Yes</strong>. The controller pulls <code>main</code> into the checkout and fetches its
	tags, and the row turns green:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/git-status-frame/git-status-after-pull.png", "Git Status panel after the pull, with the tutorial and demo rows green and reading Up to date with remote origin/main, and the example row still yellow and ahead", "large") }}
<p>The Example suite's row stays yellow, because being ahead is not something a pull resolves: the commits on
	this machine have to be pushed, or discarded, by whoever made them.</p>
<p>The pull updates the files on disk; the tests already loaded in the tree are the ones read at load time. Click
	<strong class="text-danger">Reload</strong> in the tree's action bar to load the suite again from the updated
	files.</p>

<h2 id="the-repository-in-the-results">The repository in the results</h2>
<p>The same information is recorded with every run. The results tree of the
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/details-dialog/">Details dialog</a> carries <strong>Repo</strong>,
	<strong>Branch</strong>, <strong>Commit</strong> and <strong>File Path In Repo</strong> columns for each run,
	recorded from the suite's checkout. A result can therefore always be traced to the exact commit of the test
	that produced it, which is what makes a failure reproducible after the suite has moved on.</p>

<p>The next page, <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/details-dialog/">Details Dialog</a>, opens that dialog
	at every level of the tree and walks through what it holds.</p>
